  "answer_text": "Making eye contact with someone who has a \"lazy eye\" (medically referred to as **strabismus**, or misaligned eyes) can sometimes feel socially confusing. Because people with strabismus are often highly self-conscious about their eyes during conversations, handling eye contact with warmth and ease is the best way to make them feel comfortable. \n\nHere are the most effective and polite ways to navigate eye contact:\n\n### 1. Focus on the dominant (straight) eye\nWhen someone has strabismus, one eye is typically aligned and focusing directly on you (the dominant eye), while the other eye may drift inward, outward, upward, or downward. \n* **What to do:** Simply look back at the eye that is looking directly at you. This is the eye they are actively using to see you, and focusing on it will feel like normal, direct eye contact to both of you.\n\n### 2. Look at the bridge of their nose\nIf you are having trouble focusing on just one eye, or if you feel your gaze starting to wander, use the \"nose bridge trick.\"\n* **What to do:** Focus your gaze on the bridge of their nose, right between their eyes. To the other person, this looks exactly like you are making direct eye contact, but it relieves you of the pressure of deciding which eye to look at.\n\n### 3. Use a \"soft focus\" on their entire face\nIn normal social interactions, we rarely stare intensely at a person\u2019s pupils. Instead, we look at their face as a whole.\n* **What to do:** Relax your gaze and look at their overall facial expressions, their mouth, and their gestures. This \"social gaze\" is highly natural, warm, and keeps you from hyper-focusing on their eyes.\n\n---\n\n### Key Etiquette Tips (What to Avoid)\n\n* **Avoid eye-darting:** People with strabismus are usually highly aware of where others are looking. If your eyes are constantly darting back and forth between their straight eye and their drifting eye, they will likely notice, which can make them feel uncomfortable or self-conscious. Pick a spot (either their dominant eye or the bridge of their nose) and stick with it.\n* **Do not look behind you:** If one of their eyes is drifting to the side, your brain\u2019s natural reflex might be to think they are looking at something behind you. Resist the urge to turn around and check. Remind yourself that they are actively engaged in the conversation with you.\n* **Break eye contact naturally:** You do not need to maintain continuous, unbroken eye contact. It is completely normal to look away briefly when you are thinking, speaking, or gesturing, and then look back when you are listening. \n* **Never point it out:** Unless they bring it up themselves, do not ask about their eye, make jokes, or comment on it. \n\nUltimately, the best approach is to treat them exactly as you would anyone else. Showing that you are engaged in what they are saying\u2014through nodding, smiling, and active listening\u2014is far more important than perfect eye alignment.",
